WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.980 --> 00:00:11.740
15. Class and Accent. The words and ideas you express are important, of course, but

2
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:17.880
nearly as important is the way you sound when you express them. Indeed, there are still

3
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.680
people in the world who will judge your accent before they even consider your words. You

4
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:30.420
may even have your own biases. There are a few distinct accents in the United States.

5
00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:37.200
People from the South are said to draw, speaking slowly and stretching their vowels out. Some

6
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:44.060
people find this accent reassuring. Others think it sounds unintelligent. One accent from

7
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:50.740
Southern California with very rounded vowels has been mocked in TV and movies as the dumbest

8
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:58.140
accent in America. New York and Boston accents can sound decisive or rude, depending on the

9
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:06.020
manner. In England, accents have always been very important. Even these days, children

10
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:12.200
with undesirable accents are sent to elocution lessons where they are taught how to speak

11
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:19.620
like the upper classes. Many studies show how people respond to accents. Scottish accents

12
00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:26.620
are seen as reassuring. People with accents from the Midlands of England are seen as untrustworthy

13
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:32.140
and may suffer career discrimination as a result. People from Liverpool are thought

14
00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:40.620
to sound unpleasant, while people from Ireland apparently sound sexy. In many countries,

15
00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:45.660
people with city accents may be seen as smarter, wealthier, and more accomplished than people

16
00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:52.340
who clearly sound like they are from the countryside. Your accent, of course, reveals nothing but

17
00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:59.280
where you grew up—not your intelligence, not your trustworthiness, and not your value.

18
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.340
Being able to use different accents and different situations may help people avoid prejudice,

19
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.740
but it would be better for all of us to recognize our own irrational biases and fight against

20
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:11.060
them.
